{{also|expédient}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ped-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|expedient}}, from {{der|en|fro|expedient}}, from {{der|en|la|expediens}} (stem ''expedient-),'' present participle of {{m|la|expedire|t=to bring forward, to dispatch, to [[expedite]]; impers. to be profitable, serviceable, advantageous, expedient}}, from {{m|la|ex||out}} + {{m|la|pēs|t=foot, hoof}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪkˈspiːdi.ənt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-expedient.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[suitable|Suitable]] to [[effect#Verb|effect]] some [[desired#Adjective|desired]] [[end#Noun|end]] or the [[purpose#Noun|purpose]] [[intend]]ed.
#: {{ux|en|Most people, faced with a decision, will choose the most '''expedient''' option.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|John|16|7|passage=Nevertheless I tell you the truth; It is '''expedient''' for you that I go away; for if I go not away, the Comforter willnot come unto you; but if I depart, I will send him unto you.}}
#* '''a. 1863''', {{w|Richard Whately}}, ''Thoughts and Apophthegms''
#*: Nothing but the right can ever be the '''expedient''', since that can never be true expediency which would sacrifice a greater good to a less.
# [[afford|Affording]] [[short-term]] [[benefit#Noun|benefit]], often at the [[expense#Noun|expense]] of the [[long-term]].
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=I|chapter=III|page=389|passage=[T]he judges were unanimously of opinion that [...] by the common law of England, no man, not authorised by the crown, had a right to publish political news. While the Whig party was still formidable, the government thought it '''expedient''' occasionally to connive at the violation of this rule.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=CQ Weekly|year=2009|volume=67|issue=31-36|page=2190|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=eIJWAAAAYAAJ|passage=That's because the debate pits textbook economics — which argues that bailouts beget bad behavior begets more bailouts — against practical politics. And politics, or the taking of '''expedient''' steps to keep people happy, will almost always win.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=L. Fletcher Prouty, {{w|Jesse Ventura}}| title=The Secret Team: The CIA and Its Allies in Control of the United States and the World| year=2011| url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=1616082844| passage=Government has slowly but positively moved from an active course of following plans and policies to the easier and more '''expedient''' course of the counterpuncher.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Douglas B. Klusmeyer, Demetrios G. Papademetriou| title=Immigration Policy in the Federal Republic of Germany| year=2013| url=https://books.google.com/books?isbn=0857456261| passage=Its policies toward foreign lab or across these eras reflect these sharp differences in context, but also reflect a common pattern to treat the recruitment and deployment of foreign nationals as an '''expedient''' measure to serve immediate economic objectives}}
# [[govern|Governed]] by [[self-interest]], often short-term self-interest.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|author=John Stuart Mill|title=[[s:Utilitarianism|Utilitarianism]]| passage=But the Expedient, in the sense in which it is opposed to the Right, generally means that which is '''expedient''' for the particular interest of the agent himself; as when a minister sacrifices the interests of his country to keep himself in place.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[expeditious|Expeditious]], [[quick#Adjective|quick]], [[rapid]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|II|i|lines=57–61|text=the adverse winds&nbsp;/ Whose leisure I have stay'd, have given him time&nbsp;/ To land his legions all as soon as I;&nbsp;/ His marches are '''expedient''' to this town&nbsp;/ His forces strong, his soldiers confident.}}

